  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and humid, with thunderstorm chances today\nbefore turning cooler into the first half of the weekend.\n\nReturn flow around the west side of high pressure in the western\nAtlantic and southeastern states has yielded a milder and more \nmoisture-laden air mass. Unlike previous days, diurnal showers \nand thunderstorms will be possible. The current players are: \n(1) An expansive and anomalous mid/upper ridge stretching across\nsouthern U.S./Gulf Coast up into the Ohio Valley and Mid- \nAtlantic states, (2) A sharpening trough crossing western \nOntario, and (3) An area of disturbed weather just north of the \nBahamas. In time, the high- latitude trough is expected to erode\nthe northern extent of the ridge. Although height anomalies \nremain around 1.5 to 2 sigma above mid-September climatology, \nthe subtle height falls should be enough to support increased \nlift this afternoon. This shift in the synoptic pattern allows a\nstalled front along I-70 in Ohio/Indiana to push southeastward \nlater today. The cold front is not expected to cross the entire \nregion until late tonight into early Friday morning. Thus, the \npre-frontal air mass will support scattered convection this \nafternoon and evening.\n\nWith high temperatures rising into the mid 80s to low 90s,\ncoupled with dew points reaching the upper 60s, around 1,500 to\n2,000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE develops. Deep-layer shear is\nsomewhat meager, generally averaging around 15 to 20 knots.\nHowever, forecast soundings do show some modest downdraft CAPE\naround 500 to 700 J/kg. This would be enough to produce spotty\ngusty winds in any more robust thunderstorm. Although low-level\nlapse rates sit in the 8 to 8.5 C/km range, mid-level lapse\nrates are weak which casts a doubt on how tall any storm will\nget. Convection is expected to either persist from ongoing\nactivity and/or develop off the higher terrain before pushing \ntoward the I-95 corridor during the second half of the afternoon\ninto the early evening. An instability gradient remains in place\nwith drier air to the south and east of Washington DC, so \nconvection may tend to show a weakening trend as it pushes\ntoward the I-95 corridor. Shower chances should diminish by a \ncouple hours after dark given the loss of instability.\n\nThe cold front sweeps through overnight before reaching the\nNorthern Neck of Virginia by midday Friday. With some residual\nforcing crossing the central Virginia Piedmont back into the\nPotomac Highlands, some afternoon showers and thunderstorms are\npossible. Overall, forecast highs will be in the upper 70s to\nmid 80s, with low/mid 70s for mountain locales. Winds will\nmainly be out of the north with gusts up to 15 to 20 mph. A\ncooler night lies ahead as high pressure crosses southern Quebec\ninto New England. Friday night's lows should be in the upper 50s\nto mid 60s. The influence of this ridge favors near average\ntemperatures to start the weekend on Saturday. Although skies\nshould be mostly cloudy, it is a mainly dry forecast with highs\nin the 70s to low 80s with a reduction in humidity.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather conditions return again Sunday\nand into next week.\n\nFrom Sunday and into next week, model guidence points to more \nunsettled weather, as a persistent upper ridge axis pushes towards \nthe deep South. This allows multiple upper-level disturbances to \nmove across the Mid-Atlantic from the Great Plains, driving daily \nchances for showers and thunderstorms. Long-term enemble models show \na wide range of possibilities reguarding the timing and location of \nthese disturbances. While widespread chances for showers and \nthunderstorms exist, the exact timing, coverage, and nature of these \nstorms remains quite uncertain. It will interesting to watch the \nevolution of these disturbances in the coming model runs.",
